fact WorkItem(id: "WI-872", description: some(value: "A PROVISION'S CARRIER BINDING IS MATCHED BY SHORT NAME AT DISPATCH, so a LOCAL sort that merely SHARES a provider's short name is offered that provider's impl and then refused. Against the repo's standing direction (WI-672: same_symbol DELETED; 'identity is by resolved symbol, never by last segment', spec §8.6) and against CLAUDE.md's no-short-name-comparison rule. PRE-EXISTING, MEASURED at WI-858's HEAD in two independent instances, neither involving the other: (1) `namespace p; sort Set { entity mkset(a: Int64, b: Int64) } operation f() -> Bool = eq(mkset(a:1,b:2), mkset(a:1,b:2))` — error 'type mismatch in anthill.prelude.PartialEq.eq.dispatch: expected matching impl for per-call bindings, got no impl matches', the prelude `Set`'s `provides PartialEq[T = Set]` having been offered for a carrier that is a DIFFERENT sort with the same last segment; (2) the identical program with `sort Pair` after WI-858 gave `anthill.prelude.Pair` a `PartialEq` provision. CONTROL, same file, same load: an identically-shaped `sort Duple` — a short name nothing provides for — loads and evaluates clean, so the refusal is attributable to the NAME and not to the shape or to composite equality. HOW IT SURFACED: WI-858 broke wi664_composite_eq_test, whose fixture happened to name a two-Int64 composite `Pair`; the fixture was renamed to `Duple` with the reason recorded in place, which is a workaround and not the fix. IMPACT: every short name the prelude provides an instance for (`Set`, `Map`, `List`, and now `Pair`) is effectively RESERVED against a user sort with an entity — a name-shadowing rule nobody declared, reported as an impl mismatch rather than as a collision. TWO THINGS TO SETTLE, in order: (a) WHERE the conflation is — a carrier compared by last segment somewhere on the provides-candidate path rather than by canonical symbol (`same_sort_canonical` is the correct comparator and has one owner; find the site that does not use it, by DRIVING the two reproducers above under a probe rather than by reading); (b) the DIAGNOSTIC — 'no impl matches' names neither the candidate that was offered nor the carrier it was offered for, which is why this read as a composite-equality failure for as long as it did. ACCEPTANCE: both reproducers load and evaluate; the `Duple` rename in wi664_composite_eq_test is REVERTED to `Pair` with its note deleted (that reversion IS the acceptance); a test pins a local sort per colliding prelude short name; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: CLAUDE.md 'no short-name comparison'; WI-672; docs/kernel-language.md §8.6."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-17T05:29:29Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-872", author: "user", content: "WI-1098 NARROWED THIS DEFECT AND WEAKENED THIS TICKET'S ACCEPTANCE. Read before starting.\n\nWI-1098 derives `provides PartialEq` + `provides Eq` for every TOTAL composite (an\nentity whose fields are all lawfully Eq). Both reproducers in the description above are\n`(a: Int64, b: Int64)` composites -- exactly that shape -- so each now carries its OWN\nprovision and no longer needs the prelude sort's. MEASURED at WI-1098's HEAD: a local\n`sort Pair { entity mkpair(a: Int64, b: Int64) }` and a local `sort Set` both LOAD.\n\nTHE DEFECT IS NOT FIXED. Move ONE field to `Float` -- which derives `NonEq` instead, so\nthe carrier gets no provision of its own -- and it is exactly as before, MEASURED:\n\n  local `sort Pair`  { entity mkpair(a: Float, b: Int64) }\n    => no impl matches -- unresolved: anthill.prelude.PartialEq[T = anthill.prelude.Pair.A]\n  local `sort Set`   { entity mkset(a: Float, b: Int64) }\n    => no impl matches -- unresolved: anthill.prelude.Eq[T = anthill.prelude.Set.T]\n  CONTROL `sort Duple` { entity mkduple(a: Float, b: Int64) }  => LOADS\n\nThe unresolved goal names the PRELUDE sort's own type parameter, which is the\nconflation this ticket is about, unchanged. The population it can still bite is now the\ncomposites WI-1098 leaves underivable: a parametric field, a `Float` field, a\ndispatched-eq boundary.\n\nCONSEQUENCES FOR THIS TICKET:\n - `wi858_pair_orderings_test`'s recorded-defect arm has been MOVED to the `Float`\n   shape, with the reason at the site. Its panic message still says \"delete this arm,\n   revert the Duple rename, close WI-872\" and that instruction is still right FOR THE\n   MOVED FIXTURE -- but note it fired once already on the Int64 shape, and following it\n   then would have deleted the only witness of a live defect.\n - \"the `Duple` rename in wi664_composite_eq_test is REVERTED to `Pair` ... (that\n   reversion IS the acceptance)\" IS NO LONGER SUFFICIENT EVIDENCE. wi664's `Duple` is a\n   two-Int64 composite, so reverting it to `Pair` PASSES TODAY with nothing fixed. Keep\n   the reversion as a step, but the acceptance needs a shape WI-1098 does not derive for\n   -- the three above -- or it measures nothing.", at: "2026-08-14T18:18:25Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-872", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED 2026-08-17. Full workspace green: 29 binaries, 4981 passed, 0 failed.\n\nTHE SITE, FOUND BY DRIVING (three probes, not by reading). typing.rs sort_sym_compatible\nread `local_name_of(a) == local_name_of(b)` under the comment \"handles qualified vs short\nname\". Probe 1 at collect_provides_candidates: the goal is PartialEq[T = wi872.shadow.Pair]\n(Symbol 2519, canonical 2519 -- genuinely distinct from prelude's 766) and\nanthill.prelude.Pair came back as a candidate, which REFUTES a symbol-interning collision.\nProbe 2 narrowed the accept to types_lesseq; probe 3 to that line. Replaced with\nsame_sort_canonical -- the comparator the ticket names, already the one owner. Net -7/+1.\n\nTHE TICKET'S FRAMING WAS HALF THE DEFECT. It says 'on the provides-candidate path'. It is\nthe nominal leg of types_compatible, which that path merely CALLS -- five call sites, all\nin that one relation -- so ONE branch produced TWO OPPOSITE symptoms. The refusal the\nticket describes, and, never named anywhere: at an ARGUMENT position it ACCEPTED. Two user\nnamespaces each with `sort Foo`, no prelude involved: passing a wi872.b.Foo where\nwi872.a.Foo is declared LOADED CLEAN at HEAD. A silent wrong value. That is the more\ndangerous half and no fixture covered it.\n\nWHAT THE DELETED BRIDGE WAS HOLDING UP: NOTHING. Instrumented to fire only on the bridging\ncase (symbols differ, short names agree) it logged ZERO firings across stdlib/,\nexamples/github-todo/ and anthill-todo/ -- instrument checked against the two defective\nprograms, where it fires. The suite agreed: the only test that changed verdict was the\nrecorded-defect arm firing its own 'delete this arm' panic.\n\nBACK-OUT CONTROL (stashed, rebuilt, re-driven -- not reasoned about). Local Set/Map/Pair\nat the WI-1098-underivable (a: Float, b: Int64) shape: REFUSED before, evaluate to 1 after.\nLocal List/Option/Stream/Duple: load EITHER WAY. Two-namespace Foo, bare and nested under\nList[T = ..]: LOADED before, refused after. So the ticket's IMPACT claim that every\nprelude-provided short name is reserved is TOO WIDE -- at this shape only three are, and\nthe four that pass either way are kept as the shape control and say so at the site.\n4 of the 5 new tests fail with the change backed out; the positive control passes either\nway by design.\n\nHALF (b), THE DIAGNOSTIC. Both sides render by SHORT name, so a namespace-only difference\nread `expected Foo, got Foo` and hit WI-795's cause-agnostic backstop -- 'the difference\nis in a component this diagnostic does not print; please report it', true of an unknown\ncause and wrong here, where the difference is a namespace and IS the repair. Named as a\nnew cause: 'these render alike because two different sorts share the short name `Foo` --\nexpected `wi872.a.Foo`, got `wi872.b.Foo`'. The NESTED case (List[T = a.Foo] vs\nList[T = b.Foo]) was MEASURED reachable and hits it too, which is why the walk is\nshape-agnostic rather than bare-sort_ref only.\n\nTWO REVIEW FINDINGS, BOTH REAL, BOTH IN THE NEW HELPER, NEITHER DRIVEABLE -- and the tests\nsay so rather than implying coverage. (1) The cross product could name a pair present on\nBOTH sides (one type lawfully mentioning two same-short-named sorts), blaming a\nnon-cause AND suppressing the backstop it replaces; now a symbol qualifies only if UNIQUE\nTO ITS SIDE. Not driveable: the population it guards is precisely WI-795's unnamed\nresidue. (2) load_abstract_sort registers a `sort T = ?` alias with SortKind::Sort (37\nSortAlias sources are named T after a stdlib load), so params were collected as sorts and\na T/T pair could short-circuit a real collision; now filtered by is_sort_param_symbol. I\ntried twice to drive it -- BoxX.T vs BoxY.T directly, and as a Pair[A = T] binding value --\nand both LOAD, because params compare as wildcards. Prophylactic, and recorded as such.\n\nACCEPTANCE, and where the ticket's own list was insufficient. wi664's Duple -> Pair revert\nis done and its note deleted, but the feedback was right that it proves nothing (a\ntwo-Int64 composite passes today either way) -- so the evidence is the Float shape.\nwi858's recorded-defect arm is now the positive assertion and STAYS in wi858, where the\ncost was incurred; the defect's own suite is the new\nwi872_short_name_sort_identity_test (5 tests). Spec: kernel-language.md 8.6 gains a\nparagraph putting the subtype relation's nominal leg inside the identity rule and naming\nboth symptom directions, so a future violation is recognisable from either end.", at: "2026-08-17T05:30:00Z")
